# Privacy and security rules

Reference: `docs/maintainers/03-privacy-and-security.md` and `docs/maintainers/adr/ADR-0002-*` (read-only for you).

## Non-negotiable
1. Conversation texts (drafts, incoming messages, LLM prompts and outputs) are **never persisted**: not in the database, not in Valkey (except the encrypted, single-use, short-TTL prepared result defined by the architecture), not in logs, metrics, traces, exceptions or test fixtures taken from real users.
2. Third-party metadata (forward origin, sender names, usernames, ids) is dropped at the channel adapter boundary before any use case is called.
3. C2 data (rule text, contact labels) is encrypted through the `Cipher` port before persistence. Never add a plaintext column for C2.
4. Analytics events carry the HMAC pseudonym of the user, never the raw Telegram id, and never text.
5. No external SaaS for logs, errors, analytics or LLM inference outside the approved providers. No new outbound network destination without an explicit prompt.
6. TLS verification is never disabled.

## Secrets
- Secrets only from settings (`SecretStr`). Never commit secrets, real tokens, real user data or production URLs. `.env.example` contains names and safe descriptions only.

## Input handling
- Treat every user- and partner-supplied text as untrusted. Pass it to the LLM only as a delimited data block per the prompt registry conventions; validate LLM output against its schema.
- Validate and bound all inputs (length, type, enum) at the edges.

## Required tests when touching these areas
- A log canary test: a unique marker inside processed text must not appear in any captured log event.
- Authorization tests for every endpoint/use case that accesses contacts, pairs or rules (owner, partner, stranger).
